@meta
id: 11-05
title: Design the edit so dot can do it
chapter: 11
type: lesson
skills: dot-design
par: 34
minutes: 5
vscode: click at the end of each line, type a semicolon, twelve times
open: constants.ts

@brief
A constants file that came out of a converter with every semicolon
missing. Twelve lines, twelve semicolons to add, and the lines are
different lengths so there is no straight edge to click down.

Put a semicolon at the end of each of the twelve lines.

The keys for this are all keys you already have. What matters is which
ones you choose. There is a way to say "add a semicolon to the end of
this line" that has nothing to do with where the end of the line happens
to be, and once you have said it once, saying it eleven more times is a
formality.

@keys
| `A` | append at the end of the line, wherever the cursor is |
| `Esc` | back to normal mode — this is what closes the change |
| `.` | repeat |
| `$` | end of line — you do not need it here |

@hints
Do not move to the end of the line first. Ask for an edit that already
means "at the end of the line".
--
`A` puts you in insert mode at the end of the current line from any
column. The semicolon, then `Esc`, and vim has one complete change to
remember.
--
`A;` then `Esc` on line one. After that, `j.` eleven times.

@solution
    A ; <Esc>   then  j.  eleven times

Twenty-nine keystrokes for twelve edits.

Compare the route that also works: `$a;` and `Esc`. It produces the same
file, and `.` repeats it just as happily, because the `$` is not part of
the change — the change starts at `a`. So you would be pressing `$j.`
per line, or `j$.`, and paying a key a line for nothing.

That is the shape of dot-design. Ask which of the several correct ways to
make an edit leaves the smallest, most position-independent change behind:

- `A`, `I`, `o` and `O` carry their own idea of where to go, so a repeat
  needs no aiming at all.
- An operator with a text object — `ciw`, `di(`, `ca"` — will find its own
  boundaries wherever inside them the cursor happens to land.
- An operator with a plain motion — `cw`, `d3l` — demands that the cursor
  be exactly right before you press `.`.

The first two repeat cleanly. The third works but you pay for it in
re-aiming. Reach for the first two by default and your edits become
repeatable without you thinking about it.

@start constants.ts
export const ORDER_TIMEOUT_MS = 30_000
export const REFUND_WINDOW_DAYS = 30
export const PAYMENT_RETRY_LIMIT = 3
export const BATCH_SIZE = 250
export const RECONCILE_HOUR = 2
export const RECEIPT_TEMPLATE = 'receipt-v4'
export const CURRENCY = 'GBP'
export const WEBHOOK_TOLERANCE_SECONDS = 300
export const CACHE_TTL_SECONDS = 60
export const MAX_REFUND_PENCE = 500_000
export const SUPPORT_EMAIL = 'support@example.com'
export const LEDGER_ACCOUNT = 'acct_orders'

@expected constants.ts
export const ORDER_TIMEOUT_MS = 30_000;
export const REFUND_WINDOW_DAYS = 30;
export const PAYMENT_RETRY_LIMIT = 3;
export const BATCH_SIZE = 250;
export const RECONCILE_HOUR = 2;
export const RECEIPT_TEMPLATE = 'receipt-v4';
export const CURRENCY = 'GBP';
export const WEBHOOK_TOLERANCE_SECONDS = 300;
export const CACHE_TTL_SECONDS = 60;
export const MAX_REFUND_PENCE = 500_000;
export const SUPPORT_EMAIL = 'support@example.com';
export const LEDGER_ACCOUNT = 'acct_orders';
